## Package comparison

The Phase 3 package remains aligned with the approved plan, and this round changes only the allowed focused server test. Signal interruption and healthy-peer update coverage improve, but the mandatory ownership matrix remains incomplete.

## Findings

### High — Blocked work and acceptance-vs-close still have no deterministic evidence

The coder result explicitly leaves blocked-command shutdown, blocked-sampling shutdown, and acceptance racing close outstanding. The suite does not prove interruption of a connection waiting on a command with no response after provider release, completion of server close while sampling is blocked with exact resource finalization, or the invariant that every socket accepted during shutdown is enrolled-and-finalized or synchronously destroyed. These are explicit Phase 3 gates.

### High — Exact graph, connection, and FIFO admission ownership remain incomplete

The suite still does not count one coordinator or exact finalization of the complete two-client graph. Pre-hello shutdown and partial-frame tests assert socket/path or provider state but do not count connection/listener/forwarder finalization. The two-client command test relies on concurrent Promise call order and provider call order rather than recording each socket's command-admission order into the coordinator FIFO. The package requires deterministic admission and exact ownership counts, not inference from successful facade calls.

### High — Executable cleanup failure is not exercised at the process boundary

The signal helper now has both completion and interruption cleanup tests, and the executable statically avoids the Promise facade. There is still no runtime executable-path evidence that signal-driven Layer cleanup completes in dependency order and that a close/unlink cleanup failure sets nonzero process status while preserving tagged operation/message diagnostics. Existing injected cleanup tests cover only the Promise facade or direct Layer service.

### Medium — Late-write suppression remains scheduler-dependent and conflates provider teardown

The post-hello shutdown test closes the entire graph, including the provider subscription, before emitting the late provider event. It neither counts/awaits both forwarding-fiber finalizers nor injects at a source that remains live, and it uses one `Promise.resolve()` turn as synchronization. An unchanged write counter can therefore be explained by provider teardown rather than finalized connection children and is not deterministic proof of the required no-late-write invariant.

### Medium — Socket-defect targeting is not deterministic

The defect test creates both clients concurrently and emits the injected error on `accepted[0]`, assuming that socket belongs to the variable named `broken`. That association is not established. If connection acceptance order differs, the test can kill the client it later awaits for an update. Connect sequentially or identify the accepted socket through a deterministic handshake signal before injecting the defect.

### Medium — Focused tests do not guarantee cleanup on assertion failure

The package requires every test to close scopes and sockets even when an assertion fails. The integration tests perform cleanup only at the end of their success paths; they do not use `try/finally`, test finalizers, or scoped acquisition. A failed intermediate assertion can leave a listener/socket/path alive and contaminate subsequent evidence.

